WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.140
All right.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.299
Let's start today with democratic presidential
debate number four, which took place last

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:08.109
night on CNN, a rough roughly

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:09.660
three hour affair.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:16.529
I did stream commentary for about the first
45 minutes of it before CNN shut it down.

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:21.740
Uh, Sam Cedar's stream getting shut down about
90 minutes in or so according to the tweets

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:22.880
that I got.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.369
Turns out CNN, uh, is much more aggressive
than anybody else when it comes to, uh, preventing

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:31.310
any commentary streams about their debates.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.829
But we have time today to talk about it.

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:39.340
And in general, one would expect that over
time you will have fewer participants in primary

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.340
debates.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.469
Although the democratic debates so far sort
of going in the opposite direction after getting

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:48.690
down from 20 to 10 or 11, we were back up
to 12 participants yesterday and it was a

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.980
complete and total mess, which was one of
the things that I'll talk about.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:57.000
I decided I'm not going to play any clips
from the debate in this segment.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.789
Two reasons, really.

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:03.190
One, I want to have more time for commentary
in a three hour debate.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.610
You know, there weren't really these short
moments that lend themselves to highlights

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.900
per se.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:14.229
And in any case there are a whole bunch of,
you know, 1520 minute highlight videos online

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:17.570
and CNN is being so punitive when it comes
to copyright.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:22.420
We will just be doing my thoughts on it and
uh, you know, check out the clips later.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.800
Widely available online.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.890
So let's talk about that first thing I mentioned.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:31.530
Once again, debates with 12 people on the
stage aren't good.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.149
That was again confirmed last night and I
don't want to belabor this, but yet again

00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:41.991
as if we didn't know, you don't really get
a chance for much depth when you have 12 people

00:01:41.991 --> 00:01:47.229
on stage unless you just straight up ignore
some of them for long periods of time, which

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:53.451
actually did also happen yesterday, which
is logical in a sense in that, you know, there's

00:01:53.451 --> 00:01:59.789
a big conversation right now on healthcare
for example, between the Bernie Warren view

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:06.170
and the Biden view, which is different but
then also within Bernie Warren because their

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.390
positions are also different.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:09.940
And that was a topic we'll talk about.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.250
And for awhile everybody else was just sort
of ignored.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.120
So it's like I get why they're doing that,
but at the same time, we should just have

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.250
fewer people on the stage.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:23.110
That's what would really make the most sense
now important going in, especially as we get

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:29.060
this far into the primary, we're, the positions
of the candidates are really mostly known.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:34.260
They may not be known to every voter, but
there aren't really secrets where candidates

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.150
have not yet told us their on certain issues.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:44.610
It's important to understand the context is
one where debates rarely move polling in any

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.250
significant way.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.500
And when they do, it's often not permanent.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.090
If it's up, unless you really mess up, in
which case you could do permanent damage to

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.090
yourself.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.470
And that's been the theme for the first three
debates.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:05.040
Example, first debate, the big kill shot that
got a ton of attention and play the next day

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.849
was Camila Harris going after Joe Biden.

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:10.710
Uh, that was either the first or second debate.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:16.860
It got her a spike in the polling immediately,
but then that are roaded over the 10 to 14

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:17.860
days that followed.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.709
And Kamala Harris went right back to where
she was.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:26.150
So the positive, you know, gotcha moments
only seem to work in the short term with debates

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.379
and then things regularize.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:34.150
Another example, Joe Biden, Joe Biden has
had terrible debates, mostly his first and

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.150
second, the third one.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.980
He was rather the first and third were not
very good in the second one, he actually did

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:44.069
okay because he was just attacked even in
irrational ways that made him look like a

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:45.069
martyr, quite frankly.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:47.200
And he ended up doing okay in that debate.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:54.090
Um, Joe Biden has generally performed poorly
and he has had a permanent decline in the

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.090
polls.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:04:00.239
So again, case in point, the debates tend
not to permanently move polling very much

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:01.900
unless you do poorly.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.140
So what I was interested in watching was going
to be number one, does Bernie Sanders go after

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.140
Elizabeth Warren?

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.370
We talked about the mathematical complexities
of how does Bernie get back into the running

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:12.799
for being in first place.

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:18.220
He's now pretty significantly behind both
Elizabeth Warren and Joe Biden and the complexities

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.651
mathematically are that the people that Bernie
could attack, both Biden and Warren, they're

00:04:23.651 --> 00:04:25.690
supporters prefer.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:31.890
In other words, Biden's supporters prefer
Warren over Bernie and Warren supporters prefer

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:37.580
Biden over Bernie, so by attacking them, it's
not clear that Bernie actually helps himself.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.270
So this was a question, does Bernie go after
Warren and we'll talk about that.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.390
I tweeted yesterday that he likely wouldn't.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:51.210
I said that the more likely scenario was that
Bernie would draw some mild distinctions from

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:56.120
Warren, but that nothing would really change
and this is basically how it went and we'll

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.860
get more into that momentarily.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.160
Another element I was watching for, does Joe
Biden seem more energized, not an analysis

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:11.110
of his policy, but does he seem this shrunken,
slowed down Biden or does he seem more energized?

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:15.890
He was more energized to some degree last
night compared to his just terrible prior

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:17.080
performances.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.790
Unclear that it will change very much.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:24.720
Elizabeth Warren, who now has almost [inaudible]
front runner status with Biden was definitely

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.840
more of a target, a number of people on stage
going after her, the focus of more attacks

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.760
and disagreements, which we'll get to.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.450
She weathered some of them.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:34.450
Okay.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.390
Less well with others including questions
around healthcare.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:38.680
And I'll get to that.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:44.470
We also wanted to pay attention to how do
other candidates go after or not go after

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.580
Biden on the Hunter Biden stuff.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.580
Right.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.490
Even if you don't believe the conspiracy theory
that is being parroted by Trump and the people

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:57.060
around him regarding Joe Biden and Ukraine
and that's all it is, a conspiracy theory

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:02.250
without evidence, you can still be critical
of that $50,000 a month consulting position

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.410
that Hunter Biden had with the Ukrainian gas
company.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:09.500
And there was a question as to how would the
other candidates go after Biden for that.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.010
The answer was not that much.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.540
There really wasn't that much discussion about
it.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.870
Biden did get asked about it.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:20.690
His response was pretty pathetically wishy
washy and muddled and uninspiring basically

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.340
changing the subject and not really addressing
it.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.310
Uh, and that was mostly that foreign policy.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:31.190
Foreign policy has been a weak point, not
just in the debates, but it's a weak point

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:35.380
for many of the candidates who don't really
demonstrate, I don't want to say they don't

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:39.590
have the knowledge because they may have it
somewhere in there, but they've not demonstrated

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.520
deep knowledge on foreign policy.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:47.950
Does anyone actually propose prescriptions
on foreign policy instead of just criticizing

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:49.030
Trump?

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:50.460
There wasn't that much.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:56.500
The most discussed foreign policy topic yesterday
with Syria and the Kurds, understandably,

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:02.650
almost everybody on the stage was against
the haphazard withdrawal, or at least the

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.520
way that Trump did it.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.680
They were opposed to Tulsi.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.550
Gabard sort of waffled on it.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.480
We know that this is her position.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.820
She took a position that the entire problem
is not Trump withdrawing.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:20.710
It's regime change Wars and endless Wars,
which is a major problem with American foreign

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:25.850
policy, but it's myopic and simplistic when
it comes to this particular issue as well,

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.370
but that's been her talking point on most
foreign policy issues.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.340
She staked that out.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:35.881
There's a slice of the left that likes it,
and right now it's gotten her 0.8% support

00:07:35.881 --> 00:07:37.700
from democratic primary voters.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:42.070
But other than that, either disagreement with
the way the withdrawal was done or with the

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:49.060
withdrawal, uh, a period the other candidates
did take the opportunity to distance themselves

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:50.880
from Tulsi gabardine position.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:51.880
P booted.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:55.560
Judge Frisch, for example, said that she's
just straight up wrong about it.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.650
Uh, but it was a relatively small part of
the debate of the first question.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:04.820
They made the right decision because there
were 12 people on stage to skip opening statements,

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:05.820
which I loved.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.700
They made the wrong decision to open the debate
with a question about what do you think about

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.670
impeachment, which was a complete waste of
time.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:17.800
Basically, everybody's for impeachment, Tulsi
Gabard not really for it.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.590
Pete booted, judge a little worried about
what the consequences may be, but we knew

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.680
that nothing useful was learned from that
question.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.040
It took up about 20 minutes or more.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:32.100
It provided an opportunity for some of the
candidates to grand stand and it was really,

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.100
really lame.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:38.470
Speaking of lame and cringe-worthy billionaire
Tom Stier who effectively bought his way into

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.390
the debate was totally cringe-worthy.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:43.600
His first opportunity to speak.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:47.350
He tried, he went directly to an applause
line where the audience of course has been

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:53.340
told basically to shut the F up and not make
noise and very few people reacted and it was

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.900
just downhill from there for Tom Stier contributing
nothing.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:02.390
His presence was totally superfluous in every
way and unfortunately I believe he's qualified

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:03.730
for the fifth debate.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:09.590
Elizabeth Warren had a particularly weak moment
last night and it was when she was answering

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:13.850
the question, will taxes go up under your
healthcare plan?

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:18.310
I don't want to repeat myself again and again
and again, but I've already addressed many

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:24.310
times a variety of ways in which candidates
could answer this question and she unfortunately

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:30.170
repeated some of the same talking points multiple
times, including some version of, I will not

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:35.720
sign a bill into law that doesn't lower costs
for middle class families.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.670
Sort of a strange double negative.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:42.150
And really the answer is, I mean, listen,
we know what the answer is.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:45.070
You have total healthcare costs.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:50.870
Taxes are a component of a single payer plan,
but in total costs will go down.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.600
Bernie actually handled this pretty well yesterday.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.770
Bernie has also totally missed the Mark on
this question.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:00.690
Bernie said in clear terms, total costs will
go down.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:05.980
You get rid of copays, you get rid of premiums,
you get rid of co-insurance, all of that stuff.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.620
You add a payroll tax.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:15.680
It will be mostly, it will be disproportionately
increased for corporations and for the rich.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.450
Mostly everybody will pay a little bit, but
your total costs will go down.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:24.510
It's the truth and it's a perfectly reasonable
way to do it because people's costs aren't

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.870
zero now.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:31.720
Now there's some conflict about the math of
that statement in terms of some of the claims

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:33.440
Bernie has made, and that's okay.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:34.980
We're figuring it out.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:40.370
But uh, in the end, Bernie messaged that way
better last night and Elizabeth Warren fell

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:44.760
short and she was vulnerable as a result of
it and multiple candidates saying, why can't

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.830
you just answer the question, not her best
moment last night.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.770
And what was overall a pretty good performance?

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:55.800
Andrew yangs, Andrew Yang's performance, Andrew
Yang's followers have been tweeting to me

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.740
saying, Andrew Yang destroyed it.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.150
He's about to break into the top three now.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:02.920
I didn't really see it that way.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:08.640
Andrew Yang did go after Elizabeth Warren
for not adopting his thousand dollar a month

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:10.510
universal basic income proposal.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.540
She said, well, I want to increase social
security payments.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:20.040
It was a nice attempt by Andrew Yang to, uh,
get some FaceTime with the presumptive co

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:21.230
front runner at this point.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:26.050
Elizabeth Warren, I don't think it's going
to make much of an impression or impact polling.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.240
Bernie's health came up, of course, having
a heart attack a couple of weeks ago.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:35.190
Bernie handled it as well as you can while
standing up on a stage and claiming that you're

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:36.190
fine.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:40.211
Uh, his voice and his energy seemed basically
back to normal.

00:11:40.211 --> 00:11:41.211
So that was good.

00:11:41.211 --> 00:11:44.140
I don't think he did any damage to himself
when that topic came up.

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:50.890
Uh, Covell Harris brought another one of her
gotcha attempts when she asked Elizabeth Warren,

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:54.900
will you join me in calling for Trump to be
banned on Twitter?

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:56.890
And Warren said, no.

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.670
And it was sort of an anticlimactic gotcha
moment.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.230
Harris tried to be shocked.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:04.820
No you won't, but it was a pretty weak gotcha
attempt.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:09.580
There's way bigger fish to fry and Camila
Harris has effectively done, unless like in

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:15.480
the next month she can double her support
from, you know, 5% to 10% the last question

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:17.600
of the night was also a throwaway.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:22.360
Tell us about a surprising friendship that
you have with someone.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:26.130
Real waste of time, and I know some people
in my audience will say, no, no.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:27.130
Here's why.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:28.210
That was actually an interesting question.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.210
It was a waste of time.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:32.930
I want to hear about policy overall takeaways.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:34.960
Another dog and pony show components.

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:41.090
A big part of it, relatively few new policy
details were discussed.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:47.880
I don't expect any big changes in polling
except Bernie looking generally healthy might

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:52.110
help him recover a little bit of what he's
lost in the last couple of weeks.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:55.279
Biden's seeming slightly higher energy.

00:12:55.279 --> 00:12:58.140
Maybe we'll help him recover a little bit
as well.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:03.900
No one really failed, but the people that
are polling, you know, Z one to 2%, I don't

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:07.620
see that they're going to be doing any better
as a result of this debate.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.720
Uh, Bernie did pretty well with the questions.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:15.830
He was given Biden, again, given that Biden
is the sort of slowed down Biden, he did pretty

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:18.040
well given the questions he was given.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:21.460
Although he fumbled when he was asked about
the Hunter Biden thing.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:25.940
Uh, obviously Biden supporters won't like
the content of what Bernie said.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:30.470
Bernie supporters won't like the content of
what Biden said, but they both did well enough

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:32.970
to not do damage the candidates.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:41.250
I thought overall sounded more, um, rehearsed
and less genuine than in prior debates as

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:46.770
if they're now being guided more by polling
and calculated politics.

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:50.730
Sadly, although very often that's where it
gets to at this point in the race.

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:53.900
Bottom line is that we can't keep having 12
people on the stage.

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:54.900
It's just terrible.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:58.560
I mean, what does Amy Klobuchar think about
impeachment?

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:01.250
What does Julio on Castro think about Syria?

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:04.920
What, what, why is Tulsi Gabbert not fully
behind impeachment?

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.170
I mean it's like who cares?

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:07.170
Right?

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:10.820
They have no chance and I want to get into
depth with the people that do have a shot.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:18.160
A fifth debate is on November 20th the new
qualification requirements are 3% in four

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:27.180
or more approved polls or 5% in two or more
early primary state polls and 165,000 donations.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:31.940
Now, as of right now, we are like a full month
out from that next debate.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:36.490
There are already eight people qualified for
that debate, so we may end up again with 10

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:39.000
people just not conducive to good conversation.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:45.400
Right now the people qualified for debate
number five are Biden, Sanders, Warren Harris,

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.790
Buddha, judge Tom Stier.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:51.820
Again, Andrew Yang and Cory Booker, so we
already are going to have eight people on

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:52.980
stage.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:15:00.810
Last thing, I continue to be generally concerned
about the idolatry of politicians.

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:03.330
It's not just Tulsi and yang.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:10.180
There are, you know, Bernie idolaters, Warren
idolaters from the tweets that I'm getting.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:15.670
When I asked for feedback about the Bates
or polling or whatever on Twitter, people

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:22.810
just as a few Civ about some of these candidates
as if these are saviors or near deities and

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:26.330
my position is politicians just aren't the
best period.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:31.330
These aren't, I would argue even the types
of people that we should be idolizing and

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:36.010
without commenting on any specific politicians,
I would just want to see sort of more measured

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:41.450
analysis than the gushing praise of pseudo
deities that I'm seeing on Twitter about certain

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:42.450
candidates.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:43.450
That's just me.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.980
